Learning outcomes
On successful completion of the module students will be able to:

carry out regression analysis with generalised linear models including the use of link functions;
understand the use of deviance in model selection;
appreciate the problems caused by overdispersion;
fit and interpret the special cases of log linear models and logistic regression;
compare methods for scatterplot smoothing suitable for use in a generalised additive model;
interpret a fitted generalised additive model;
describe the characteristic features of survival data including censoring;
use a range of parametric and semi-parametric regression models to assess the effects of covariates on a survival distribution; and
use a statistical package with real data to fit these models to data and to write a report giving and interpreting the results.

13.4 The smoothing matrix
Here we show, for a given value of the smoothing parameter \(\lambda\) and the index \(\nu \geq 1\), that the fitted value \(\hat{f}_\lambda(x_k)\) at each data location \(x_k\) may be written as a linear combination of the observations, \(y_1,\dots,y_n\).
Recall from Proposition 12.3 that the smoothing spline \(\hat{f}_\lambda(x)\), which minimises the penalised sum of squares criterion Equation 12.2 for a given value of the smoothing parameter \(\lambda\), has coefficients \(\hat{\mathbf{a}}, \hat{\mathbf{b}}\) where \[
\begin{bmatrix}
\hat{\mathbf a} \\ \hat{\mathbf b}
\end{bmatrix}
=
M_{\lambda}^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
{\mathbf 0} \\ {\mathbf y}
\end{bmatrix}
\quad \text{where} \quad
M_{\lambda} =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & L_\nu \\
L_\nu^T & K_\nu+\lambda^* I_n
\end{bmatrix}.
\]
The fitted values of the smoothing spline at the data locations can be represented in matrix form as \[
\mathbf{\hat{f}} = \left[
\begin{array}{c}\hat{f}(x_1) \\
\vdots  \\
\hat{f}(x_n)\end{array}
\right]
= K_\nu  \, {\hat {\mathbf b}} + L_\nu \, {\hat {\mathbf a}}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
K_\nu & L_\nu
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} M_\lambda^{12} \\ M_\lambda^{22}
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{y},
\] where \(M_\lambda^{-1}\) has been partitioned in the form \[
M_\lambda^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} M_\lambda^{11} & M_\lambda^{12} \\ M_\lambda^{21} & M_\lambda^{22}
\end{bmatrix},
\] where \(M_\lambda^{12}\) is \(\nu \times n\) and \(M_\lambda^{22}\) is \(n \times n\).
It can be shown that the matrix \[
S_\lambda =
\begin{bmatrix} K_\nu & L_\nu
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
M_\lambda^{12} \\ M_\lambda^{22}
\end{bmatrix}
\] is a symmetric positive definite matrix for \(\lambda &gt; 0\), called the smoothing matrix. Then \(S_\lambda\) connects the data \(\mathbf{y}\) to the fitted values through \[
\mathbf{\hat f} = S_\lambda \, \mathbf{y}.
\tag{13.4}\] That is, the fitted values are a simple linear function of the data.

